      }
    }
  ],
  "keyInsight": "The Frasch process creatively uses nested pipes and fluid dynamics to physically extract sulfur, delivering heat and lift simultaneously without requiring underground access.",
  "reconstructionNotes": "A vertical 2D cross-section. Draw the three concentric pipes with clear annular gaps. Use distinct colors for the flows (e.g., blue arrows for water, gray for air, orange/yellow for the sulfur mixture) to make the concurrent pathways readable. The pipe assembly extends from an above-ground manifold through a brown 'Soil' layer into a yellow 'Solid sulfur deposit'. At the bottom, show the liquid sulfur as a brighter pool. The cutaway must clearly show the staggered pipe lengths: outer pipe delivers water into the top of the pool, inner pipe delivers air to the very bottom, and middle pipe captures the rising mixture."
}

